The reduction family: multiple accumulator chains and an interleaved exponential (lab proposal)

A reduction over a row with one accumulator is one chain of dependent operations. On the v79 a
result is ready two packets after its producer, thus each step costs about four cycles where the
unit could retire one every half packet, and the packets stay almost empty. This is the measured
signature of RMS_NORM on the phone: 1.63 instructions per packet.

The same shape costs far more in softmax, whose inner operation is the f32 exponential: a chain
of about eighteen dependent operations, thus about 130 cycles for each vector of 32 lanes, which
the lab measures as 137. Four independent chains cover the latency.

This proposal changes the shape and not the precision. Softmax stays in f32 throughout, and the
interleaved exponential runs the same steps with the same constants in the same order as
hvx_vec_exp_f32 of hvx-exp.h, thus each lane gives the same value. The norms gain four partial
accumulators, which changes the order of the sum and thus its last bits.

